## Proof

Let $f:[0,1]\to[0,\infty)$ be continuous and strictly increasing. The region $R$ is bounded by $x=0,\,x=1,\,y=0,\,y=f(x)$.

**Centroids.** The $x$-coordinate of the centroid of a lamina with density $\rho(x)$ along $[0,1]$ is $\dfrac{\int_0^1 x\,\rho(x)\,dx}{\int_0^1 \rho(x)\,dx}$.

- For the planar region $R$, the area element at $x$ is $f(x)\,dx$, so
$$x_1=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,dx}{\displaystyle\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx}.$$

- For the solid obtained by rotating $R$ about the $x$-axis, the volume element at $x$ is a disk of radius $f(x)$, i.e. $\pi f(x)^2\,dx$, so
$$x_2=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2\,dx}{\displaystyle\int_0^1 f(x)^2\,dx}.$$

Set $A=\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx>0$ and $B=\int_0^1 f(x)^2\,dx>0$. We must show $x_1<x_2$, equivalently
$$\left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,dx\right)B \;<\; \left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2\,dx\right)A,$$
i.e. the quantity
$$I \;:=\; \left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2\,dx\right)A \;-\; \left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,dx\right)B$$
is strictly positive.

**Rewriting $I$ as a double integral.** Expanding the products of single integrals as double integrals over $[0,1]^2$:
$$I=\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2 f(y)\,dy\,dx \;-\; \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,f(y)^2\,dy\,dx
=\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,f(y)\bigl[f(x)-f(y)\bigr]\,dy\,dx.$$

Swapping the dummy variables $x\leftrightarrow y$ gives the same value
$$I=\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 y\,f(x)\,f(y)\bigl[f(y)-f(x)\bigr]\,dx\,dy.$$

Adding the two representations and dividing by $2$:
$$I=\frac12\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 f(x)\,f(y)\,(x-y)\bigl[f(x)-f(y)\bigr]\,dx\,dy.$$

**Sign of the integrand.** Since $f$ is strictly increasing, for $x\neq y$ the factors $(x-y)$ and $\bigl(f(x)-f(y)\bigr)$ have the same sign, so
$$(x-y)\bigl[f(x)-f(y)\bigr]>0 \qquad (x\neq y).$$
Also $f(x),f(y)\ge 0$, hence the integrand is non-negative everywhere on $[0,1]^2$.

**Strict positivity.** It remains to show the integral is not merely zero. Because $f$ is strictly increasing and maps into $[0,\infty)$, we have $f(1)>f(0)\ge 0$, so $f(1)>0$. By continuity there exists $\delta>0$ with $f(x)>0$ for all $x\in(1-\delta,1]$. On the square $(1-\delta,1]^2$ the factor $f(x)f(y)$ is strictly positive, and $(x-y)[f(x)-f(y)]>0$ whenever $x\neq y$. Thus the integrand is strictly positive on a set of positive (two-dimensional) measure. A non-negative continuous integrand that is strictly positive on a set of positive measure has strictly positive integral. Therefore
$$I>0.$$

**Conclusion.** $I>0$ is exactly $\left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,dx\right)B < \left(\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2\,dx\right)A$, which (dividing by the positive $AB$) is $x_1<x_2$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
